{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1785216322,"nonce":"9sfzD0I/nWVefUHhjJk3qwq9TGE=","message":"**Implemented** — closing.\n\nThe change-set reader now resolves `trusted.overlay.redirect` instead of refusing it. `Verdict::Directory` carries the recorded old path, resolved into **parent-snapshot** coordinates, and `ChangeSet::grafts()` yields `(from, to)`. Refusals are kept exactly where guessing would be unsafe: a redirect on a non-directory, `opaque` and `redirect` on the same directory, and malformed values (empty, `..`, `.`, repeated or trailing separators, NUL).\n\nThree things fell out that were not in this ticket and are now in ADR-0062 part 3:\n\n- **Grafts must be resolved before deletions.** A rename also whiteouts the old path, so applying deletions first deletes the graft's own source.\n- **Every path except `redirect` is a merged-view path**, including the renamed directory's own — its upper name IS its new name. Mixing the coordinates silently grafts the wrong subtree.\n- **Redirects compose.** A relative redirect under an already-renamed ancestor resolves against that ancestor's LOWER coordinate. Resolving against the merged one grafts nothing and loses a subtree without erroring.\n\nOne deliberate deviation from how this ticket was written: an **absolute** redirect is resolved, not rejected. A leading `/` is the kernel's encoding for overlay-root-relative, which is how a cross-parent rename is recorded — rejecting it would refuse precisely the renames this ticket exists to support. It cannot escape the tree once `..` is banned.\n\nAlso fixed here: `Unsupported::Metacopy` was quoting the retracted measurement (\"mounts and then copies the data up anyway\") in a user-facing error. It now cites the real reason — `nfs_export=on,metacopy=on` is refused as conflicting options.","files":null}]}